        "reduce-no-shows": "---\nslug: reduce-no-shows\nname: Cut no-shows\nline: Deposits, easy cancelling and a short waitlist.\nsearch: how to reduce no-shows\ntitle: How to reduce no-shows at your salon or clinic\ndescription: \"How to reduce no-shows: four simple changes that make clients turn up, or tell you in time when they can't.\"\nh1: How to reduce no-shows at your salon or clinic\nlead: An empty slot costs you the time and the money. Four simple changes that make people turn up, or tell you in time when they can't.\npublished: 2026-10-24\nupdated: 2026-10-24\ngroup: Bookings and no-shows\ntrades: [salons, clinics]\nauthor:\n  name: Saroh team\n  role: Writer\nquestions:\n  - question: Will a deposit put clients off?\n    answer: Most clients don't mind a small deposit for a long service. Ask for it only where a no-show costs you most.\n  - question: What should I do when someone doesn't turn up?\n    answer: Mark it, send a short friendly message, and ask for a deposit next time.\ntoConfirm:\n  - the author's name and role\n  - the hero picture\n---\n\n## Remind them the day before\n\nA short message the day before gives a client the chance to tell you they can't come. Send it yourself in the evening, when people check their phones.\n\n| When | What to send |\n|---|---|\n| The day before | Your time, the service and how to change it |\n| Two hours before | A one-line nudge |\n\n## Ask for a deposit on long services\n\nA deposit makes a booking feel real. Keep it small, and say what happens to it if they cancel:\n\n- **Long services:** a deposit of a quarter is enough.\n- **Short services:** no deposit; a reminder does the job.\n\n> **In Saroh** you can ask for a deposit when a client books. See [how bookings work](/help/bookings).\n\n## Make cancelling easy\n\n1. Put your cancel rule in the booking message.\n2. Give a number or a link to cancel.\n3. Thank people who cancel in time.\n\n## Keep a short waitlist\n\nWhen someone cancels, offer the slot to the first person on your list. A list of three or four names is enough.\n",
